# Stage 2: Visual Evidence for Campaign Health Assessment

In Stage 1, we built a three-dimensional health score model (Cost Efficiency 40%, Conversion Quality 35%, Competitiveness 25%) to identify high-risk campaigns. This section provides visual evidence to support the findings, focusing on the distribution of health scores, risk patterns across dimensions, and trends over time.

## 1. Health Score Distribution by Risk Level

![Health Score Distribution by Risk Level](health_score_distribution.png)

The stacked histogram shows the distribution of health scores colored by risk level (Low, Medium, High, Critical). The majority of campaigns fall into the low‑risk category with health scores below 50, while a long tail of high‑risk campaigns appears beyond 60. This reinforces Stage1’s conclusion that the health score effectively separates campaigns into distinct risk tiers, enabling prioritization of interventions.

## 2. Health Score vs. ROI with Cost Indication

![Health Score vs ROI (size = total cost)](health_vs_roi.png)

Each point’s size represents the campaign’s total cost, and color indicates risk level. The strong positive relationship between health score and ROI confirms that the model’s cost‑efficiency component aligns with actual performance. High‑cost campaigns that appear in the lower‑right quadrant (high ROI but low health score) may suffer from poor conversion quality or competitiveness—highlighting the need to look beyond ROI alone.

## 3. Average Health Score by Campaign Type

![Average Health Score by Campaign Type](health_by_campaign_type.png)

Shopping campaigns have the lowest average health scores, while Search and Display campaigns score higher. This visual validates Stage1’s finding that Shopping campaigns are disproportionately high‑risk, likely due to intense competition and lower conversion quality. Video and Performance Max campaigns show moderate scores, suggesting that channel‑type‑specific optimization strategies are necessary.

## 4. Risk‑Level Distribution Across Industries

![Risk Level Distribution Across Industries](risk_by_industry.png)

Stacked bars show the proportion of campaigns in each industry that belong to each risk level. Real Estate and E‑commerce contain the highest share of Critical‑risk campaigns, whereas SaaS and Education are dominated by Low‑risk campaigns. This reinforces Stage1’s recommendation that industry‑specific competitive dynamics should inform budget allocation and bidding strategies.

## 5. Monthly ROI Trend by Risk Level

![Monthly Average ROI by Risk Level](monthly_roi_by_risk.png)

Time‑series lines track the average ROI for each risk level over the 18‑month window. High‑ and Critical‑risk campaigns exhibit volatile ROI with a declining trend, while Low‑risk campaigns maintain stable, positive returns. This supports Stage1’s observation that high‑risk campaigns are not only under‑performing but also becoming more unstable over time—a signal that urgent intervention is required.

## 6. Correlation Heatmap of Health Score Components

![Correlation Heatmap of Health Score Components](correlation_heatmap.png)

The heatmap shows strong positive correlations between conversion quality, cost efficiency, and competitiveness scores, confirming that the three dimensions of the health model are mutually reinforcing. ROI is most tightly correlated with cost‑efficiency score, while impression share correlates with competitiveness. This validates the weighting choice (40% Cost Efficiency, 35% Conversion Quality, 25% Competitiveness) used in Stage1.

## Conclusion

These visualizations confirm the key Stage1 findings:
- The health score model successfully stratifies campaigns by risk.
- High‑cost, low‑conversion risk campaigns are concentrated in Shopping campaigns and in Real Estate/E‑commerce industries.
- ROI alone is insufficient; the composite health score provides a more holistic view.
- Time‑series trends show deteriorating performance for high‑risk campaigns, underscoring the urgency of the optimization recommendations provided in Stage1.
```
