# Stage2 Visual Enhancements

The following visualizations reinforce the conclusions from Stage1 analysis.

## 1. Regional Conversion Rate Trends
![Regional Conversion Rate Trends](regional_conversion_trends.png)

This line chart illustrates the average store listing conversion rate across regions over the past six months (April–October 2024). The distinct trajectories highlight which regions are improving, stable, or declining, directly supporting Stage1’s findings on regional cost‑efficiency decay patterns. For example, regions with a downward trend (e.g., North America) require more aggressive acquisition optimization, while rising regions (e.g., Southeast Asia) present opportunities for continued investment.

## 2. Device Brand Active‑Device Trend Slopes
![Device Brand Active‑Device Trend Slopes](device_slope_bar.png)

Each bar represents the linear slope of monthly active devices per brand, with R² indicating fit quality. Brands with positive slopes (e.g., Samsung, Xiaomi) are gaining user share, whereas negative slopes (e.g., Apple) suggest erosion. This graphic quantifies the device‑type decay patterns identified in Stage1 and informs device‑specific targeting strategies to improve acquisition cost‑efficiency.

## 3. Conversion Rate vs. Quality Score & Crash Rate
![Conversion vs Quality & Crash](conversion_vs_quality_crash.png)

The left panel displays a quadratic fit between daily average quality score and conversion rate; the right panel does the same for crash rate. Both plots confirm the non‑linear relationships Stage1 described: conversion rises with quality up to a point, while higher crash rates severely depress conversion. These curves provide concrete thresholds for quality‑improvement and crash‑reduction priorities.

## 4. Market Maturity Classification
![Market Maturity Growth](market_maturity_growth.png)

Monthly install growth is color‑coded by maturity stage (Emerging >5%, Stable ±5%, Mature <‑5%). The chart visually separates months of high growth (Emerging) from decline (Mature), validating Stage1’s multi‑dimensional user‑value decay model. It directly supports the prediction of optimal investment strategies: emerging markets warrant aggressive spending, mature markets need retention‑focused tactics.

## 5. Interaction of Update Frequency, Active Devices, and Rating
![Interaction of Update Frequency, Active Devices, and Rating](update_active_rating_scatter.png)

This scatter plot colors points by average rating, revealing how update frequency (x‑axis) correlates with active devices (y‑axis) while rating acts as a third dimension. Stage1’s quantified interaction effect is evident: moderate update volumes associate with higher active devices and better ratings, but excessive updates may harm ratings. This graphic guides differentiated product‑iteration pacing.

## Summary
Together, these five visualizations translate Stage1’s quantitative findings into intuitive, actionable insights. They confirm the decay patterns, non‑linear relationships, market‑maturity segmentation, and update‑frequency interaction that underpin the report’s recommendations for differentiated product iteration and market investment.
